 a market ready fda-approved product  so it can actually start saving lives at  this station they're measuring how  quickly vetti gel wants blood so whole  blood versus blood with our product as  you can see we're already getting that  natural spike we've already caught it in  a really short period of time so what  does this mean  for its application in the real world  let's say there was you know a soldier  who was shot on the battlefield he has  three minutes to live he's bleeding out  of his femoral artery the next leading  competitor can stop a bleed and say five  to ten minutes but he's only got those  three you apply this in 15 seconds time  you can ensure that not only will the  bleeding stop but it'll remain stopped  due to the high cloud strength  vetti gel is about to make its real  world debut as a product for the  veterinary market and it will have to  prove safe and effective for animals if  it's ever going to be approved for human  use but Joe fully expects that his  technology will eventually become  ubiquitous in healthcare really our goal  is to get this in every ambulance and  every soldier's belt and in every mom's  purse and really the in every mom's  purse means having a product that is  easy enough to be used but I just spent  anybody the folks behind Betty Gel know  that they have a long road ahead of them  any new medical breakthrough does it's  going to be a ton of time in a mountain  of paperwork before what they're  devising can be used on human beings but  should that time come what we see here  isn't just some esoteric tool that can  be added to a surgeon's kit this might  just be the band-aid of the future  [Music]

